{
 "description": "PRState.ApplyDeployEvent used to drop any deploy-phase event on a same-epoch merged PR whose carried head was neither the stored head nor the merge SHA, and a merge never repaired a stale head, so a PR that merged while PRObject's head was stale (a missed synchronize) dropped every rollout progress and deploy_success event and sat in ListRollouts forever (aerial#2308, g3-shadow-soak-r9: PRObject head c00b710c, row head 701c341c, merge 913c2325; three HandleRolloutProgress and HandleDeploySuccess drop_stale while legacy marked the row deployed). Once the merge SHA is known the merge commit is the post-merge identity: an event carrying it applies, a different merge commit drops stale, and an event with no merge SHA drops only when its head is in PriorHeads. A same-merge closed event or an applied deploy-phase event carrying a real head adopts it (PriorHeads, RepoObject SHA index); a merge-only ref never does. Case 1: register at c00b, squash-merge push (head = merge 913c), closed (head 701c, merge 913c), kustomization requested, then the legacy watcher completes aerial-core and aerial-config (the shadow sends carry the row head 701c and merge 913c) and deploy_success. PRObject lands deployed on head 701c with c00b in PriorHeads, DeployedJobs == row deployed_jobs, the projection agrees with the row, ListRollouts is empty, and the deploy_success effect legacy records as intended equals the one the restate-owned replay publishes. Case 2: the closed event is lost; the first rollout progress repairs the head instead, same outcome. Case 3 (contrast): a deploy event naming a different merge commit drops stale. Case 4 (contrast): a deploy event with no merge SHA whose head is in PriorHeads drops stale.",
